        "prefix_token_count": 53,
        "step_number": 1,
        "text": "Step 1: Let the number of sides of the convex polygon be \\( n \\). The sum of the interior angles of an \\( n \\)-sided polygon is given by the formula \\( (n-2) \\times 180^\\circ \\)."
      },
      {
        "aligned_char_end": 478,
        "boundary_aligned_back": true,
        "char_end": 479,
        "char_start": 180,
        "effective_step": 2,
        "end_token_index": 149,
        "near_end": false,
        "prefix_token_count": 150,
        "step_number": 2,
        "text": "Step 2: According to the problem, the sum of all but one of its interior angles is \\( 1070^\\circ \\). Therefore, if we let the missing angle be \\( x \\), then the sum of all interior angles minus this one angle is \\( 1070^\\circ \\). This can be written as:\n\\[ (n-2) \\times 180^\\circ - x = 1070^\\circ \\]"
      },
      {
        "aligned_char_end": 838,
        "boundary_aligned_back": true,
        "char_end": 839,
        "char_start": 481,
        "effective_step": 3,
        "end_token_index": 287,
        "near_end": false,
        "prefix_token_count": 288,
        "step_number": 3,
        "text": "Step 3: Since \\( x \\) is an interior angle of a convex polygon, it must be less than \\( 180^\\circ \\). We can rearrange the equation to solve for \\( x \\):\n\\[ x = (n-2) \\times 180^\\circ - 1070^\\circ \\]\nSince \\( x \\) is an interior angle, it must be between \\( 0^\\circ \\) and \\( 180^\\circ \\). Therefore:\n\\[ 0 < (n-2) \\times 180^\\circ - 1070^\\circ < 180^\\circ \\]"
      },
      {
        "aligned_char_end": 1116,
        "boundary_aligned_back": true,
        "char_end": 1117,
        "char_start": 841,
        "effective_step": 4,
        "end_token_index": 423,
        "near_end": false,
        "prefix_token_count": 424,
        "step_number": 4,
        "text": "Step 4: First, solve the left part of the inequality:\n\\[ 0 < (n-2) \\times 180^\\circ - 1070^\\circ \\]\n\\[ 1070^\\circ < (n-2) \\times 180^\\circ \\]\n\\[ \\frac{1070^\\circ}{180^\\circ} < n-2 \\]\n\\[ 5.9444 < n-2 \\]\n\\[ 7.9444 < n \\]\nSince \\( n \\) must be an integer, we have:\n\\[ n \\geq 8 \\]"
      },
      {
        "aligned_char_end": 1401,
        "boundary_aligned_back": true,
        "char_end": 1402,
        "char_start": 1119,
        "effective_step": 5,
        "end_token_index": 563,
        "near_end": false,
        "prefix_token_count": 564,
        "step_number": 5,
        "text": "Step 5: Now, solve the right part of the inequality:\n\\[ (n-2) \\times 180^\\circ - 1070^\\circ < 180^\\circ \\]\n\\[ (n-2) \\times 180^\\circ < 1250^\\circ \\]\n\\[ n-2 < \\frac{1250^\\circ}{180^\\circ} \\]\n\\[ n-2 < 6.9444 \\]\n\\[ n < 8.9444 \\]\nSince \\( n \\) must be an integer, we have:\n\\[ n \\leq 8 \\]"
      },
      {
        "aligned_char_end": 1503,
        "boundary_aligned_back": true,
        "char_end": 1505,
